{"lecture_id":"2026-07-14-M2-5","lecture_summary":"The lecture develops rolling-without-slipping kinematics and energy methods, then introduces angular momentum, its relationship to external torque, and its conservation. It applies these ideas to a hollow sphere rolling down a ramp and to rotationally inelastic interactions involving rain entering rotating cups and a bullet embedding in a cylinder.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Rotational Motion Physics, Basic Introduction, Angular Velocity & Tangential Acceleration","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQ9AH2S8B6Y","reason":"Builds the rolling-kinematics foundation: period, angular velocity and acceleration, and rotational–translational relationships underlying v = ωR and a = αR."},{"rank":2,"title":"Rotational Kinetic Energy and Moment of Inertia Examples & Physics Problems","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=REIP2mf6sIQ","reason":"The strongest match for the hollow-sphere ramp method: apply energy conservation to rolling objects on inclines while including rotational kinetic energy and moment of inertia."},{"rank":3,"title":"Angular Momentum - Basic Introduction, Torque, Inertia, Conservation of Angular Momentum","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzjIMuf-yuo","reason":"Provides the conceptual framework linking linear and angular momentum, external torque, rotational inertia, and angular-momentum conservation."},{"rank":4,"title":"Angular Momentum Physics Practice Problems","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QghXDDJtJeQ","reason":"Adds representative calculations with disks, rods, and a child joining a merry-go-round—the closest playlist analogue to added or sticking mass changing a rotating system’s inertia and angular speed."}],"coverage_gaps":["No strong match explicitly covers the cycloid traced by a rim point or derives the no-slip condition from circumference and period.","The rolling-energy video’s metadata does not explicitly cover the thin-shell result I = 2mR²/3 or explain why ideal static friction enforces rolling without dissipating mechanical energy.","No listed description explicitly covers particle angular momentum as r × p, including its sin φ dependence, or a tangential bullet embedding in a cylinder.","No exact match covers rain entering rotating cups or explicitly calculates thermal/internal-energy gain in a rotational sticking collision; the merry-go-round problem is the closest analogue."]}
